If you've looked at Shopify's theme library, you'll have noticed they're not bad. Some of them look genuinely professional. So the question is legitimate: why pay for a custom-designed ecommerce website when you can buy a polished template for £100–£300?

It's a fair question and it deserves a proper answer — not a reflexive "custom is always better" from a design agency with an obvious interest in selling you custom work.

The honest answer is: it depends on what you need your store to do and what your business is trying to be.

## What you get with a Shopify theme

A Shopify theme is a pre-built design that you configure to your products, brand colours, logo, and content. The structure — how pages are laid out, where images appear, how the navigation works — is determined by the theme. You work within it.

Good themes are well-designed, well-tested across devices, and built to handle standard ecommerce scenarios. For a business with straightforward products and no strong brand differentiation, a good theme is a perfectly reasonable starting point.

The trade-offs are in three areas: brand fit, conversion optimisation, and differentiation.

## Brand fit

A theme is designed to work for any business. It's deliberately generic. That's a feature when you want quick setup — and a limitation when your brand has a strong visual identity, a specific type of customer, or a premium positioning that a generic layout undermines.

Consider two businesses side by side: a luxury artisan chocolatier and a budget surplus tool retailer. If both are using the same Shopify theme with different colour palettes, the underlying experience is the same. The layout, the information hierarchy, the way products are presented — identical.

For the surplus tool retailer, that might be fine. Customers are buying on price and practicality. But for the chocolatier, where the customer experience — the feeling of buying something indulgent and special — is part of what they're paying for, a generic theme actively undermines the brand.

A custom-designed store is built around your specific brand identity. Typography, spacing, how product photography is displayed, how copy is written into the layout, how the checkout flows — all of it is designed intentionally for your products and your customers.

## Conversion optimisation

This is where the meaningful commercial difference lies. A theme is built for the average store. Your store isn't average — it has specific products, a specific customer, and specific moments in the shopping journey where people drop off.

A custom design can address those moments directly. If your analytics show that 40% of customers abandon at the cart page, a custom solution can redesign that page specifically. If your products benefit from detailed comparison tables, a custom layout can build them in. If video is important for demonstrating your products, the design can centre it.

Templates are optimised for the general case. Custom design is optimised for your case.

This matters more as your store volume grows. A store processing 10 orders a month doesn't feel the commercial impact of a 5% conversion rate improvement. A store processing 500 orders a month very much does.

## Differentiation

In competitive product categories, standing out matters. If five businesses in your niche are using the same Shopify theme — which does happen — the distinguishing factor is price, reviews, and reputation. The store experience itself is identical.

A custom-designed store looks and feels like your brand, not like every other store on the same platform. That distinctiveness contributes to brand recognition, trust, and the perception that you're an established, professional business rather than a side project someone set up last week.

## When a theme is the right choice

Templates make sense in specific situations:

- You need to launch quickly and will invest in a custom build once revenue is established
- Your products are functional and buyers make decisions on specification rather than brand experience
- Your budget genuinely doesn't allow for custom work right now
- You're testing a business idea before committing to a full build

In these cases, a solid theme with good product photography and well-written descriptions will outperform a custom build that's rushed or under-resourced.

## When custom is worth it

Custom design is the right investment when:

- Your brand positioning or product quality justifies premium presentation
- You're in a competitive market where the experience of buying matters
- You have specific layout or functionality requirements the theme doesn't support cleanly
- You're planning to invest in driving traffic — through [SEO](/services/local-seo-google-ranking/), social, or advertising — and you want the site to convert that traffic as effectively as possible

If you're spending money getting people to your store, you want the store to do its job. A custom design does that job better than a theme because it's built specifically for your customers rather than for the generic Shopify user.

## What "custom" means in practice

Custom doesn't mean built from scratch in code, at enormous expense. At NC Digital, our [ecommerce websites](/services/ecommerce-development/) are custom-designed on Shopify or WordPress/WooCommerce — we use the platform's infrastructure but build the design, layout, and experience from the ground up around your brand.

